@page "/about"

<PageTitle>About</PageTitle>

<nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="">Home</a>
    <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
    <span aria-current="page">About</span>
</nav>

@* Headed with the tool's own mark and name rather than the word About, which the breadcrumb and
   the tab already say. Memoria's own whatever the header shows: an Administrator's branding
   names this deployment, and this page is about what it runs. alt="" because the name follows,
   and a screen reader naming the mark as well would say Memoria twice. *@
<h1 class="product"><img class="product-mark" src="@Assets["logo.png"]" alt=""/>Memoria</h1>
<p class="lede">
    Memoria Web is the browser tool of the Memoria framework: it reads a store, and shows the events
    appended to it and the models built from them. This page is what the tool itself is — the
    version running, where it came from, and where the rest of the framework is written up.
</p>

<h2 id="build">Build</h2>
@* Drawn the way a model's Info tab draws the facts about a stored row — the label beside the
   value on one card — because that is what these are: the facts about the tool, read the same
   way. Not reached by a tab, so the heading above it is written rather than clicked, and the panel
   is named by it just the same. Both values are taken from the assembly rather than written here,
   so a release changes them without anyone remembering to. *@
<section class="tab-panel" aria-labelledby="build">
    <dl class="facts">
        @* Linked to the release notes as a whole rather than to a heading for this version: the
           notes are one page, and a link to an anchor that a release note was not given would
           land at the top of it anyway. *@
        <dt>Version</dt>
        <dd><a href="@ReleaseNotes" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">@Running.Version</a></dd>

        @* The commit the build was stamped with, linked to itself on GitHub — the one thing that
           tells two builds of the same version apart. A build made outside a checkout has none,
           and says so rather than linking nowhere. *@
        <dt>Commit</dt>
        <dd>
            @if (Running.Commit is null)
            {
                <span class="missing">not stamped on this build</span>
            }
            else
            {
                <a href="@($"{Repository}/commit/{Running.Commit}")" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">@Running.ShortCommit</a>
            }
        </dd>

        <dt>Licence</dt>
        <dd><a href="@($"{Repository}/blob/main/LICENSE.md")" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RPL 1.5 or the Memoria Commercial Licence</a></dd>
    </dl>
</section>

<h2 id="documentation">Documentation</h2>
@* The tiles the overviews are entered by, each a page of the documentation and each opened in a
   tab of its own, because they leave the tool. The tool's own pages first, then the framework it
   reads for; the release notes last, because they are what the version above is looked up in. *@
<ul class="tiles" aria-labelledby="documentation">
    <li>
        <a href="@(Docs + "tools/memoria-web.html")" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            <SectionMark Kind="SectionKind.Documentation"/>
            <span class="body">
                <span class="name">Memoria Web</span>
                <span class="detail">What it is, and what each page shows.</span>
            </span>
        </a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a href="@(Docs + "tools/memoria-web-configuration.html")" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            <SectionMark Kind="SectionKind.Documentation"/>
            <span class="body">
                <span class="name">Configuration</span>
                <span class="detail">The connection string, and the settings around it.</span>
            </span>
        </a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a href="@(Docs + "tools/memoria-web-deployment.html")" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            <SectionMark Kind="SectionKind.Documentation"/>
            <span class="body">
                <span class="name">Deployment</span>
                <span class="detail">Running it somewhere other than localhost.</span>
            </span>
        </a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a href="@(Docs + "tools/memoria-web-samples.html")" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            <SectionMark Kind="SectionKind.Documentation"/>
            <span class="body">
                <span class="name">Sample data</span>
                <span class="detail">Filling a store to try it against.</span>
            </span>
        </a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a href="@Docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            <SectionMark Kind="SectionKind.Documentation"/>
            <span class="body">
                <span class="name">Memoria</span>
                <span class="detail">The framework: the mediator, event sourcing and the stores.</span>
            </span>
        </a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a href="@ReleaseNotes" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            <SectionMark Kind="SectionKind.Documentation"/>
            <span class="body">
                <span class="name">Release notes</span>
                <span class="detail">What changed in each version.</span>
            </span>
        </a>
    </li>
</ul>

<h2 id="licensing">Licensing</h2>
@* The two pages of the documentation the licence row in the build is decided by, drawn as the
   tiles above are and opened in a tab of their own for the same reason. Last on the page, where a
   reader looks for the terms, and after the documentation most come here for. The terms first,
   because they say which licence applies; the prices second, because they matter only once the
   commercial one does. *@
<ul class="tiles" aria-labelledby="licensing">
    <li>
        <a href="@(Docs + "license.html")" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            <SectionMark Kind="SectionKind.Documentation"/>
            <span class="body">
                <span class="name">Licence</span>
                <span class="detail">RPL 1.5 or the commercial licence, and which one you need.</span>
            </span>
        </a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a href="@(Docs + "pricing.html")" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            <SectionMark Kind="SectionKind.Documentation"/>
            <span class="body">
                <span class="name">Pricing</span>
                <span class="detail">Who pays nothing, and what each edition costs.</span>
            </span>
        </a>
    </li>
</ul>

@code {

    private const string Repository = "https://github.com/lucabriguglia/Memoria";
    private const string Docs = "https://lucabriguglia.github.io/Memoria/";
    private const string ReleaseNotes = Docs + "release-notes.html";

    /// <summary>Read once for the page: the assembly does not change while it is loaded.</summary>
    private static readonly Build Running = Build.OfRunningApplication();

}
